          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. Does the classification link the two, and what target does guidance describe for someone like me?",
          "answer": "**Does the classification link the two?** Yes, in coding terms. The ICD-10-CM coding summary (coding-hypertension-i10) says that hypertension with chronic kidney disease is classified as a combination code, I12, with a second code for the stage of kidney disease. The same summary says the classification reads \"with\" as \"associated with\" and presumes the link unless the record says the conditions are unrelated (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11). The chapter summary for kidney disease (coding-icd10-chapter-14-genitourinary) notes that chronic kidney disease is subdivided by stage. This describes how records are coded. It does not tell anyone what is true about your body, and only your clinician can make that judgment.\n\n**What blood pressure target does guidance describe?** The retrieved passages do not give a target for people with kidney disease. The diagnosis guidance (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets) sets out how office readings are classified, but the passage that was returned stops before it states the target. The coronary artery guidance (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention) mentions a goal below 130/80 mm Hg \"for most people,\" but it applies to people with coronary artery disease, not to kidney disease. That same guidance lists renin-angiotensin blockade as a treatment consideration where chronic kidney disease is also present, but it does not name a blood pressure target for kidney disease.\n\nAsk your kidney specialist or primary care clinician which blood pressure goal fits your situation. Do not change any medicine based on this summary.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
